### What are sealed classes?

A sealed class or interface restricts exactly which types may extend or implement it. You declare it with the `sealed` keyword and list the permitted subtypes with `permits`. The compiler then knows every possible subtype, which enables exhaustive `switch` expressions without a `default` branch.

In a banking API, you might model the types of fee strategies as a sealed interface so no rogue implementation can slip in.

```java
package com.bank.model;

public sealed interface FeeStrategy
        permits RegularFeeStrategy, PremiumFeeStrategy, ZeroFeeStrategy {
    java.math.BigDecimal calculateFee(java.math.BigDecimal amount);
}

public record RegularFeeStrategy() implements FeeStrategy {
    public java.math.BigDecimal calculateFee(java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        return amount.multiply(new java.math.BigDecimal("0.005"));
    }
}

public record PremiumFeeStrategy() implements FeeStrategy {
    public java.math.BigDecimal calculateFee(java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        return amount.multiply(new java.math.BigDecimal("0.002"));
    }
}

public record ZeroFeeStrategy() implements FeeStrategy {
    public java.math.BigDecimal calculateFee(java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        return java.math.BigDecimal.ZERO;
    }
}
```

Because the interface is sealed, a `switch` over it is exhaustive and the compiler warns if you forget a case.

### What are records and why use them for DTOs?

A `record` is a special class introduced in Java 16 that is an immutable data carrier. The compiler generates the canonical constructor, accessor methods (not getters with `get` prefix, just the field name), `equals`, `hashCode`, and `toString` from the component list in the header.

Records are ideal for REST API response and request DTOs because they are concise, immutable, and self-documenting.

```java
package com.bank.dto;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public record AccountResponse(
        String accountId,
        String holderName,
        BigDecimal balance
) {}

// Usage:
AccountResponse resp = new AccountResponse("SAV2001", "Rajesh", new BigDecimal("150000.00"));
System.out.println(resp.accountId());  // SAV2001
System.out.println(resp);             // AccountResponse[accountId=SAV2001, holderName=Rajesh, balance=150000.00]
```

```text
SAV2001
AccountResponse[accountId=SAV2001, holderName=Rajesh, balance=150000.00]
```

### What is pattern matching and how does it simplify API code?

Pattern matching combines type checking and variable binding in one expression. Before Java 16, you had to use `instanceof` and then cast. With pattern matching the cast is eliminated.

```java
// Before Java 16 – manual cast:
if (strategy instanceof RegularFeeStrategy) {
    RegularFeeStrategy reg = (RegularFeeStrategy) strategy;
    System.out.println("Regular fee: " + reg.calculateFee(amount));
}

// Java 16+ – pattern matching instanceof:
if (strategy instanceof RegularFeeStrategy reg) {
    System.out.println("Regular fee: " + reg.calculateFee(amount));
}
```

In Java 21+, sealed interfaces combine with pattern matching in `switch` expressions for exhaustive, cast-free branching.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class FeeService {
    public BigDecimal computeFee(FeeStrategy strategy, BigDecimal amount) {
        return switch (strategy) {
            case RegularFeeStrategy r  -> r.calculateFee(amount);
            case PremiumFeeStrategy p  -> p.calculateFee(amount);
            case ZeroFeeStrategy z     -> z.calculateFee(amount);
            // No default needed – sealed permits list is exhaustive
        };
    }
}
```

### How do modules (JPMS) enforce encapsulation?

The Java Platform Module System, introduced in Java 9, lets you declare explicit boundaries for a JAR. A `module-info.java` file states which packages are exported and which modules are required. Internal packages that are not exported are truly hidden at both compile time and runtime.

```java
// module-info.java for the banking service module
module com.bank.service {
    requires java.sql;
    requires com.fasterxml.jackson.databind;
    exports com.bank.dto;       // public API – DTOs
    exports com.bank.service;   // public API – services
    // com.bank.model is NOT exported – internal only
}
```

This means another module cannot access `com.bank.model.Account` even if the class is public, because the package is not exported. This is stronger than package-private access in the pre-module world.

### What is reflection and how do frameworks use it?

Reflection is the ability to inspect and manipulate classes, methods, and fields at runtime using the `java.lang.reflect` API. Frameworks like Spring and Jackson rely on reflection to read annotations, invoke methods, and populate fields without knowing the types at compile time.

```java
package com.bank.util;

import java.lang.reflect.Field;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ReflectionDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        var resp = new com.bank.dto.AccountResponse("SAV2001", "Rajesh", new BigDecimal("150000"));

        // Reflection reads fields at runtime:
        for (Field f : resp.getClass().getDeclaredFields()) {
            f.setAccessible(true);
            System.out.println(f.getName() + " = " + f.get(resp));
        }
    }
}
```

```text
accountId = SAV2001
holderName = Rajesh
balance = 150000
```

The risk is that reflection bypasses compile-time safety and encapsulation. With JPMS modules, strong encapsulation blocks reflective access to unexported packages unless you explicitly `opens` them.

### How do sealed classes differ from enums?

An enum has a fixed set of singleton instances with no per-instance data variation. A sealed class has a fixed set of subtypes, but each subtype can carry different fields, have its own methods, and be instantiated multiple times.

| Feature | Enum | Sealed class |
|---|---|---|
| Fixed set of types | Yes (instances) | Yes (subtypes) |
| Per-instance fields | Limited | Full freedom |
| Instantiation | Singleton per value | Many instances per subtype |
| Switch exhaustiveness | Yes | Yes (Java 21+) |
| Inheritance | Cannot extend | Full subtype hierarchy |

```java
// Enum – fixed values, no per-instance data:
public enum AccountType { SAVINGS, CURRENT, LOAN }

// Sealed – fixed subtypes, each can carry data:
public sealed interface TransactionResult
        permits Success, InsufficientFunds, AccountFrozen {}
public record Success(String txnId, BigDecimal newBalance) implements TransactionResult {}
public record InsufficientFunds(BigDecimal available, BigDecimal requested) implements TransactionResult {}
public record AccountFrozen(String reason) implements TransactionResult {}
```

### What are the limitations of records?

Records cannot extend another class because they implicitly extend `java.lang.Record`. Their fields are final, so you cannot mutate them after construction. They cannot declare instance fields outside the component list. If you need mutable state, inheritance, or non-trivial field logic, use a regular class.

```java
// This does NOT compile – records cannot extend another class:
// public record LoanResponse() extends BaseResponse {}

// Workaround – records CAN implement interfaces:
public sealed interface ApiResponse permits AccountResponse, ErrorResponse {}
public record AccountResponse(String id, BigDecimal balance) implements ApiResponse {}
public record ErrorResponse(String code, String message) implements ApiResponse {}
```

### What are common mistakes with these features?

A common mistake is forgetting to list a permitted subtype in the `permits` clause. The compiler will reject the unlisted type.

```java
// Wrong – MissingStrategy not in permits list:
public sealed interface FeeStrategy
        permits RegularFeeStrategy, PremiumFeeStrategy {}
public record MissingStrategy() implements FeeStrategy { // COMPILE ERROR
    public BigDecimal calculateFee(BigDecimal a) { return BigDecimal.ZERO; }
}
```

Another mistake is using reflection to mutate record fields. Even though `setAccessible(true)` can bypass the `final` modifier, mutating a record breaks its contract and can cause `equals` and `hashCode` to return inconsistent results.

```java
// Wrong – never mutate a record via reflection:
Field f = resp.getClass().getDeclaredField("balance");
f.setAccessible(true);
f.set(resp, new BigDecimal("999999")); // breaks immutability contract
```

### When should you use each feature in API design?

Use sealed interfaces to model a closed set of domain outcomes or strategies where the compiler must enforce exhaustiveness, such as transaction results or fee tiers. Use records for DTOs that cross the API boundary, because they are immutable, concise, and work well with Jackson serialization. Use pattern matching to dispatch on sealed subtypes cleanly in service logic. Use modules in large banking monorepos to enforce that internal packages stay internal. Use reflection only through frameworks; avoid hand-rolled reflection in application code because it bypasses type safety.

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer understands that sealed classes plus records plus pattern matching form a complete algebraic data type system in Java. This lets you model a transfer result as `sealed interface TransferResult permits Success, InsufficientFunds, Frozen`, return it from the service, and handle every case exhaustively in the controller without a default branch, which means the compiler catches missing cases at build time rather than at runtime. In regulated banking this compile-time guarantee is a significant safety improvement over unchecked downcasts or string-based status codes.

Senior engineers also know that Jackson supports records natively since version 2.12, so `record AccountResponse(String id, BigDecimal balance) {}` serializes to JSON without any additional configuration. And they know that JPMS strong encapsulation can break frameworks that rely on deep reflection, which is why Spring Boot adds `--add-opens` flags during startup.
